# 17. Benchmarks need to compare architecture, not model

This is critical.

ISO-Bench found that agents sharing underlying models can differ substantially, which supports the idea that scaffolding itself materially affects performance. ([arXiv][12])

AgentArch similarly found different architectural configurations interacting differently with models rather than one universal architecture winning everything. ([arXiv][13])

And recent security-agent research specifically warns that comparisons often unfairly change both the underlying model and architecture, making it hard to know whether improvements came from the scaffold. ([arXiv][14])

So your benchmark system needs matrices:

```text
                  MODEL
             M1    M2    M3
ARCH A       67    74    82
ARCH B       71    69    84
ARCH C       63    80    78
```

And:

```text
same model
same budget
same tools
same benchmark
different architecture
```

This becomes hugely valuable.

